From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Alsey Coleman Miller <alseycmiller@gmail.com>
Date: Tue, 28 Jul 2026 00:00:00 -0400
Subject: [PATCH] Build BoringSSL without assembly on unsupported architectures

swift-crypto's vendored BoringSSL only provides perlasm-generated assembly for
x86_64 and aarch64 (and Windows); every other target -- including 32-bit arm,
i386 and powerpc64le -- hits a hard message(FATAL_ERROR ...) with no way to
fall back to a portable build.

Replace that fatal error with the pure-C BoringSSL configuration by defining
OPENSSL_NO_ASM for the CCryptoBoringSSL target. This is a supported BoringSSL
build mode: the C implementations are always compiled and the assembly is only
an optimization guarded by !defined(OPENSSL_NO_ASM). Targets that do have
assembly (x86_64, aarch64) are unaffected.
---
 Sources/CCryptoBoringSSL/CMakeLists.txt | 6 +++++-
 1 file changed, 5 insertions(+), 1 deletion(-)

--- a/Sources/CCryptoBoringSSL/CMakeLists.txt
+++ b/Sources/CCryptoBoringSSL/CMakeLists.txt
@@ -356,3 +356,7 @@
 else()
-   message(FATAL_ERROR "platform sources are not defined here for ${CMAKE_SYSTEM_NAME} on ${CMAKE_SYSTEM_PROCESSOR}")
+  # No perlasm-generated assembly is vendored for this architecture. Build the
+  # portable pure-C implementation instead of failing. OPENSSL_NO_ASM is a
+  # supported BoringSSL configuration; the assembly is only an optimization
+  # guarded by !defined(OPENSSL_NO_ASM).
+  target_compile_definitions(CCryptoBoringSSL PRIVATE OPENSSL_NO_ASM)
 endif()
